.Dd July 3, 2026
.Dt XTC_BLOCKING 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_blocking_run ,
.Nm xtc_blocking_run_off_loop ,
.Nm xtc_blocking_submit ,
.Nm xtc_blocking_pool_size ,
.Nm xtc_blocking_shutdown
.Nd offload blocking work to a thread pool without stalling the loop
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_blocking.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_blocking_run "int (*fn)(void *)" "void *arg" "int *out_result"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_blocking_run_off_loop "int (*fn)(void *)" "void *arg" "int *out_result"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_blocking_submit "int (*fn)(void *)" "void *arg"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_blocking_pool_size "int nthreads"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_blocking_shutdown "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A loop thread must never block in a syscall: doing so stalls every
other process sharing that loop.  Some work is unavoidably blocking
however -- reads and
.Xr fsync 2
on regular files (which are not pollable),
.Xr getaddrinfo 3 ,
and third-party libraries.  The
.Nm xtc_blocking
facility runs such a call on a dedicated pool thread and parks the
calling process until it finishes, so the loop keeps running other
work meanwhile.  The wakeup reuses the runtime's existing machinery:
the pool thread signals completion on a pipe the calling process waits
on with
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd ,
so no new scheduler integration is required.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_blocking_run
runs
.Fa fn Ns ( Ns Fa arg Ns )
on a pool thread and parks the caller until it completes, storing
.Fa fn Ns 's
return value in
.Fa *out_result .
It must be called from within a process or coroutine running on a
loop; called outside that context (or where the offload cannot be set
up) it runs
.Fa fn
synchronously on the current thread -- always correct, just not
yielding.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_blocking_run_off_loop
is the counterpart for a caller that is a plain OS thread and
.Em not
a fiber (its
.Fn xtc_self
is none): it offloads
.Fa fn Ns ( Ns Fa arg Ns )
to the pool and blocks the calling thread on the completion pipe with
a real
.Xr read 2 ,
rather than parking a fiber (which a bare thread cannot do), so
.Fa fn
runs on a pool worker instead of inline on the caller.
It remains synchronous from the caller's point of view -- it blocks
the calling thread until
.Fa fn
completes -- and it does
.Em not
shorten any lock the caller holds across the call: moving a
.Xr fsync 2
off the caller thread does not shorten a lock held around it.
It is a building block for a group-commit or batching design (submit
once, wait once, wake many), not a way to let a non-fiber caller do
other work during the call: to keep serving other multiplexed tasks
during a blocking call, make those tasks fibers on a loop and use
.Fn xtc_blocking_run ,
which yields.
It returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
if
.Fa fn
is
.Dv NULL
or if it is called from a fiber (use
.Fn xtc_blocking_run
there); on a pool or pipe setup failure it runs
.Fa fn
inline and returns
.Dv XTC_OK .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_blocking_submit
is the fire-and-forget variant: it hands
.Fa fn Ns ( Ns Fa arg Ns )
to the pool and returns immediately without waiting for or collecting
the result.  It never parks, so it is callable from any context (for
example prefetch or read-ahead).  The caller owns
.Fa arg Ns 's
lifetime until
.Fa fn
runs (or has
.Fa fn
free it); there is no completion signal.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_blocking_pool_size
pins the pool to a fixed
.Fa nthreads
worker threads, overriding the automatic default.  It must be called
before the first
.Fn xtc_blocking_run
or
.Fn xtc_blocking_submit ;
a later call returns
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
(too late).  By default the pool auto-sizes: it starts with a
CPU-scaled worker count
.Pf ( Fn max 4 ", online CPUs" ,
capped at 64) and grows on demand up to 64 when work queues faster
than idle workers can take it, so the offload path is neither an
artificial bottleneck on a large host nor over-provisioned on a small
one.  An explicit size disables growth and fixes the pool at exactly
that many threads.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_blocking_shutdown
stops the pool, joining its threads.  It is idempotent, for orderly
shutdown and leak-checked test runs.  A new
.Fn xtc_blocking_run
after shutdown restarts the pool.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_blocking_run
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
once
.Fa fn
has run.
.Fn xtc_blocking_submit
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
if the work was enqueued, or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code.
.Fn xtc_blocking_pool_size
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
if called after the pool has started.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_aio 3 ,
.Xr xtc_async 3
.Sh AUTHORS
.An The XTC Project .
